WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.009
“Something which has never occurred since
time immemorial; a young woman did not fart

00:00:06.009 --> 00:00:14.900
in her husband's lap.” That was the world’s
oldest recorded joke, first told in 1900 BC

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:21.779
in what is now southern Iraq. Obviously, toilet
human was just as popular back then as always.

00:00:21.779 --> 00:00:28.359
The joke itself may not be very funny now,
but in 1900 BC it would have caused people

00:00:28.359 --> 00:00:33.730
to crease up with laughter. But why? How come
when we’re told a joke or when someone falls

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:39.329
over or when we’re just in an awkward situation
– this strange gargled, grunting noise comes

00:00:39.329 --> 00:00:49.340
out your mouth? What on earth is laughter
and why do we do it? Let’s find out.

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:56.289
Laughter is the most universal form of communication.
Unlike language which can just as easily divide

00:00:56.289 --> 00:01:02.399
people, laughter brings people together. And
unlike language, everyone in the world understands

00:01:02.399 --> 00:01:09.020
laughter. And when I say everyone I mean everyone,
because human beings are born with the ability

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:14.159
to laugh, we don’t learn to laugh, it’s
a basic human instinct.

00:01:14.159 --> 00:01:19.850
Laughter is powerful, it can create powerful
bonds between individuals. Laughter is often

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:24.520
a part of the human mating ritual as well.
And some people even believe laughter can

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:31.210
heal you. Oh, and laughter can also be a force
for evil, but we’ll get to that later.

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:36.119
One of the most surreal aspects of laughter
is that, apart from fake laughter, we don’t

00:01:36.119 --> 00:01:42.490
decide when to laugh, it just comes naturally.
This is strong evidence that there’s a good,

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:48.619
causational reason why humans laugh - such
a natural and instinctive trait doesn’t

00:01:48.619 --> 00:01:50.979
just come along by accident.

00:01:50.979 --> 00:01:55.810
But here’s the kicker. What if I told you
that laughing has nothing to do with a person’s

00:01:55.810 --> 00:02:00.590
sense of humour? When we laugh it’s not
just because the other person told us a joke

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:07.280
that fits in with our taste in comedy, in
fact we laugh for a completely different reason.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:12.420
Laughter isn’t about humour, it’s all
about being social, it’s about fitting in

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:14.470
and accepting others.

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:19.470
In a study that was carried out over a ten-year
period it was observed that most laughter

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:26.330
does not follow a joke. In fact, the researchers
observed that when a joke is told the person

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:31.780
telling the joke is much more likely to laugh
that the recipients of the joke. The vast

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:37.099
majority of laughter actually follows simple
statements and questions such as “Hey John,

00:02:37.099 --> 00:02:42.480
where ya been?” and “How did you do on
the test?”. They don’t sound particularly

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:47.860
funny and that’s because they’re not.
In these situations, people laugh, not because

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:52.470
the statement is funny but because it helps
to strengthen social bonds.

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:57.890
Laughter is a means of acceptance of what
the other person is saying – a way of demonstrating

00:02:57.890 --> 00:03:02.940
that you’re both “on the same page”,
without having to use a single word. Laughter

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:08.519
is a way to show the other person that you
appreciate what they’re telling you. It

00:03:08.519 --> 00:03:14.390
can demonstrate that you like someone and
is sometimes used to diffuse awkward situations.

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:20.230
You’re thirty times more likely to laugh
when other people are around than when you’re

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:26.299
alone. Which just goes to show that laughter
is much more about bonding than humour. You’re

00:03:26.299 --> 00:03:31.750
also more likely to laugh when you’re with
your friends as opposed to strangers. Because

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:34.299
you’re more inclined to bond with your friends.

00:03:34.299 --> 00:03:40.799
It’s believed that laughter evolved from
the panting behaviour of our primate ancestors.

00:03:40.799 --> 00:03:47.060
If you tickle an ape or gorilla, they will
produce an exuberant panting noise. That noise

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:53.099
is a primate’s version of laughter. But
not all animals laugh, so far we’re only

00:03:53.099 --> 00:03:58.659
aware of two animals that laugh, apes and
rats. But there may be others that share this

00:03:58.659 --> 00:04:02.810
ability, so get out there and start tickling
random creatures.

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:08.099
It’s believed the reason that primates evolved
the ability to laugh is because it was an

00:04:08.099 --> 00:04:13.299
effective means of communication and a way
to strengthen bonds with their fellow beings,

00:04:13.299 --> 00:04:16.250
long before the inception of language.

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:21.600
Apes can often be seen tickling each other
whilst playing, all the while laughing, although

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:28.340
from our perspective it would appear to be
joyful panting. If you’ve ever observed

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:33.290
this behaviour you will notice that before
long the entire troop of apes will be merrily

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:37.759
panting in unison. This is because laughter
is incredibly contagious.

00:04:37.759 --> 00:04:42.870
In fact, a great way to make someone laugh
is just start laughing yourself. Although

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:49.850
this works remarkably better if your own laughter
is real, not faked. The human brain is unbelievably

00:04:49.850 --> 00:04:55.090
good at telling the difference between fake
laughter and real laughter.

00:04:55.090 --> 00:05:01.340
Laughter is so contagious that in 1962 in
Tanzania there was a laughter epidemic. It

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:06.830
all started when three girls started laughing
in a school in Kashasha. Before long the entire

00:05:06.830 --> 00:05:12.150
school was erupting in fits of laughter. It
was so bad that the school had to close. The

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:16.970
laughter soon spread to neighbouring villages
and before long it had affected hundreds of

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:23.300
people. The laughing plague lasted for a staggering
eighteen months after is started. In total

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:28.860
over a thousand people were infected with
non-stop laughing for days on end and people

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:35.500
reported symptoms such as pain, flatulence,
attacks of crying and random screaming. Laughter

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:38.000
sure is a powerful thing.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:44.820
But with great power comes great responsibility.
For laughter can be used for evil. It’s

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:50.750
true, there’s a dark side to laughter. Remember
when bullies would laugh in the playground

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:56.310
whilst causing another person upset and misery.
That’s because scientists believe there

00:05:56.310 --> 00:06:01.680
are two kinds of laughter. The first is spontaneous
laughter, that’s the kind when we laugh

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:07.000
with our friends or when someone tells us
a joke. The other kind of laughter, non-spontaneous

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:13.090
laughter, includes fake laughter, nervous
laughter and evil cackling, the kind of laughter

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:16.160
you might hear from a villain in a James Bond
film.

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:21.060
Psychologists believe that this kind of laughter
is used to show superiority and dominance

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:27.729
over another. When militants and gangs attack
people they have often been witnessed to laugh

00:06:27.729 --> 00:06:34.699
whilst doing it. But why? Someone being violently
attacked is certainly no laughing matter.

00:06:34.699 --> 00:06:40.530
It’s believed that laughter is used in such
circumstances to form a bond between the attackers

00:06:40.530 --> 00:06:46.770
and to exclude the victim, making them feel
isolated and therefore frightened. Laughter

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:50.759
helps to assert the attacker’s dominance
and makes them feel more powerful.

00:06:50.759 --> 00:06:59.110
So to summarise, when we laugh we are accessing
and utilising an ancient evolutionary communication

00:06:59.110 --> 00:07:05.280
system, that not only helps to create relationships
with other humans but to also maintain and

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:11.599
strengthen them. We can also use it to regulate
emotions, show dominance over others and even

00:07:11.599 --> 00:07:12.800
to create fear.

